On 17-Oct-2005 Takashi Iwai wrote:

>> I'm trying to create a virtual volume control that acts as
>> a Line Playback Volume (or master, if you prefer). I modified
>> control_hw.c in order to intercept r/w operations on the
>> following controls:
>>
>> PCM out volume
>> Monitor mixer
>> Virtual volume
>>
>> On writes to PCM-out it has to write the values modified by vvol.
>> That's ok, but how can I make alsa-lib to not deliver the events
>> about PCM out when they are generated by a write to vvol ?
>
> I don't understand the question well, especially the relation among
> the controls above.  Which controls are "real" controls and which
> are virtual?  What kind of behavior is expected by each?

They all become virtual because the real values of hw controls are not
available for the user anymore. The user accesses to v_controls instead.
"Virtual volume" (v_vol) is the software control I wish to add, without
removing pcm and monitor volumes. All gains are expressed in dB. pcmvol and
monitor (without the v_) are the real hw controls. Briefly, I want to do the
following:


v_vol.read(out) {
	return v_vol.gain[out]
}


v_vol.write(out, gain) {
	v_vol.gain[out]=gain;

	/**** do not deliver monitor and pcmout events here !! ****/

	// v_vol acts as a master volume, so we tweak all output gains to simulate it
	for-each-input {
		monitor.write(out, in, v_monitor.gain[out][in] + v_vol.gain[out]);
	}
	pcmvol.write(out, v_pcmvol.gain[out] + v_vol.gain[out]);
}


v_pcmvol.read(out) {
	return v_pcmvol.gain[out];
}


v_pcmvol.write(out, gain) {
	v_pcmvol.gain[out]=gain;
	pcmvol.write(out, v_pcmvol.gain[out] + v_vol.gain[out]);
}


v_monitor.read(out, in) {
	return v_monitor.gain[out][in];
}


v_monitor.write(out, in, gain) {
	v_monitor.gain[out][in]=gain;
	monitor.write(out, in, v_monitor.gain[out][in] + v_vol.gain[out]);
}


